[APPLAUSE]

00:16:08.940 --> 00:16:11.480
EVAN ANDERSON: Thanks.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:15.710
That was a great overview of
both conversational commerce

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:18.830
and where that's going, as well
as some of the business value

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:23.450
and how it's really
going into production.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:27.620
And as well, as Becky
had a great slide

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.590
about how to integrate
these sorts of experiences

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:32.210
into your existing
infrastructure,

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:36.075
because how many people are
building their new enterprise

00:16:36.075 --> 00:16:38.450
stuff completely Greenfield,
without needing to integrate

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.840
with any existing databases?

00:16:41.840 --> 00:16:43.130
I see one hand in the back.

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:46.690
Congratulations, you
are the lucky one.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:52.340
But otherwise, building
this as a facade in front

00:16:52.340 --> 00:16:55.250
with clear enterprise
APIs is a great way

00:16:55.250 --> 00:16:59.070
to get into this,
and experiment,

00:16:59.070 --> 00:17:01.070
and move quickly, without
destabilizing the rest

00:17:01.070 --> 00:17:03.140
of your infrastructure.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:06.319
So I'm a software engineer,
as I said at the beginning.

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:09.339
I like to see how
stuff is built.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:13.750
And so we're going to start
talking about how it's built

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:18.940
and a little bit about why
I think that Serverless

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:20.819
is the great place to do this.

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:26.950
So at the end, I will point
you to the GitHub repo

00:17:26.950 --> 00:17:32.560
where you can go and take
a look, and copy the code,

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:35.610
and look at all the stuff I
did, and ask me questions about,

00:17:35.610 --> 00:17:38.950
why is your Python code
so hastily written?

00:17:38.950 --> 00:17:42.190
And the answer is, it's a demo.

00:17:42.190 --> 00:17:48.470
So to review the request flow,
this is very similar to before.

00:17:48.470 --> 00:17:50.890
I talk to Google on my device.

00:17:50.890 --> 00:17:52.450
It calls the Assistant.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:55.930
The Assistant calls
Dialogflow, because this is not

00:17:55.930 --> 00:17:58.870
about training your own
natural language model.

00:17:58.870 --> 00:18:00.850
There's lots of people
who've done this before,

00:18:00.850 --> 00:18:03.310
and Google has a
pretty good agent.

00:18:03.310 --> 00:18:05.710
And then at the end,
I want to hook this up

00:18:05.710 --> 00:18:08.850
with some Python code, because
it's fast and easy to write.

00:18:08.850 --> 00:18:10.600
I don't have to spend
a lot of time on it,

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:15.670
and it's kind of fun to
get to see how far Python

00:18:15.670 --> 00:18:18.190
has gone since I wrote it
seriously in production five

00:18:18.190 --> 00:18:20.360
or 10 years ago.

00:18:20.360 --> 00:18:23.260
So this is a Google conference.

00:18:23.260 --> 00:18:26.560
We've got lots of places
where you can run Python,

00:18:26.560 --> 00:18:28.420
also lots of places
where you can answer

00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:30.500
this stuff that's not Google.

00:18:30.500 --> 00:18:34.210
So all the way on the left,
you've got on-premise.

00:18:34.210 --> 00:18:36.610
Maybe you've got an existing VM.

00:18:36.610 --> 00:18:39.490
All you need to do is set
that up, image the disk,

00:18:39.490 --> 00:18:42.280
talk to your IT department about
firewalls and network access.

00:18:42.280 --> 00:18:43.735
So Google can call it--

00:18:43.735 --> 00:18:45.520
ah, it doesn't sound
quite so much fun.

00:18:45.520 --> 00:18:48.370
So Google Compute Engine--

00:18:48.370 --> 00:18:52.870
if you like having VMs,
just pop out a credit card,

00:18:52.870 --> 00:18:54.580
and you can get a VM.

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:58.002
And when you're done with
it, just throw it away.

00:18:58.002 --> 00:18:59.710
You still have to
handle a lot of scaling

00:18:59.710 --> 00:19:02.860
and replication
yourself and still need

00:19:02.860 --> 00:19:05.350
to deal with OSes and patches.

00:19:05.350 --> 00:19:08.380
Well, it's a little bit
more agile, a little bit

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:11.230
less infrastructure work,
because you can just

00:19:11.230 --> 00:19:15.640
show up one day, and you don't
have to procure and space plan.

00:19:15.640 --> 00:19:17.540
But let's keep
going up the stack

00:19:17.540 --> 00:19:19.490
to more managed solutions.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:22.900
Kubernetes Engine is next.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:26.170
In Kubernetes Engine, Google
manages the OS and a lot

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:27.400
of the other components.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:29.630
And you simply tell
Kubernetes, hey,

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:31.210
this is the container
I want to run.

00:19:31.210 --> 00:19:32.470
Please run five copies.

00:19:32.470 --> 00:19:35.290
Put a load balancer in front.

00:19:35.290 --> 00:19:37.390
If you've ever used
Kubernetes before,

00:19:37.390 --> 00:19:40.960
you can declare all of this
stuff in a YAML format.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:44.290
You'll spend a lot of time
making friends with YAML.

00:19:44.290 --> 00:19:47.180
And you'll get to see how
all the pieces fit together,

00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:50.960
which is great if you like
fitting pieces together.

00:19:50.960 --> 00:19:53.770
We're still focused
on, OK, I just

00:19:53.770 --> 00:19:55.520
want to get my code to run.

00:19:55.520 --> 00:19:59.200
So you'll see that we have
the three compute Serverless

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.270
offerings up here.

00:20:01.270 --> 00:20:07.260
Cloud Functions is great for
single HTTP endpoint requests

00:20:07.260 --> 00:20:09.730
where you're only going to
get one request at a time.

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:13.660
App Engine is a full web
application environment.

00:20:13.660 --> 00:20:17.050
So for Python, I'm
going to use Flask.

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:19.930
For Node, you could use Express.

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:22.190
You're going down get a
very well-trod path there.

00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:24.700
But if you want to just
handle a single JSON request,

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.110
there's going to be a
little bit more overhead.

00:20:26.110 --> 00:20:27.280
Those first two are GA.

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:30.340
If you're building something
for production today,

00:20:30.340 --> 00:20:32.560
use the GA ones.

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.780
The last one is new,
something we're announcing

00:20:34.780 --> 00:20:37.000
at this conference, Cloud Run.

00:20:37.000 --> 00:20:39.970
It is a container-based
Serverless environment.

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:43.390
So you can take a container
that runs on your desktop,

00:20:43.390 --> 00:20:46.570
send it up into the cloud,
and Google will scale it.

00:20:46.570 --> 00:20:49.480
Google will give you SSL--

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:51.770
SSL on all three, by the way.

00:20:51.770 --> 00:20:53.600
That's not a Cloud
Run exclusive feature,

00:20:53.600 --> 00:20:56.560
but you can use any
language you want.

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.270
The first two are
language-specific.

00:20:59.270 --> 00:21:02.920
So there are four
languages, I think,

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:05.380
supported for Cloud Functions.

00:21:05.380 --> 00:21:08.980
About six languages and
two or three versions

00:21:08.980 --> 00:21:12.040
of each language on App Engine.

00:21:12.040 --> 00:21:14.230
But I see Mike Helmick here.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:15.925
If you want to
write it in Elixir,

00:21:15.925 --> 00:21:17.800
then you're going to
build your own container

00:21:17.800 --> 00:21:19.000
and do the last thing.

00:21:19.000 --> 00:21:20.560
If you want to do
Rust or something

00:21:20.560 --> 00:21:22.690
like that, again, the last one.

00:21:22.690 --> 00:21:26.340
And I want to point out--
if you've got simple needs,

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.543
Dialogflow actually
is a managed service

00:21:29.543 --> 00:21:31.210
where you can do all
of your programming

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.220
in Dialogflow, as
long as you don't

00:21:33.220 --> 00:21:35.110
need to do something
complicated,

00:21:35.110 --> 00:21:38.440
like access that database.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:40.930
So we can't use the
fully managed product

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:45.440
because we've got a database
that we want to do--

00:21:45.440 --> 00:21:47.450
we've got existing
enterprise things.

00:21:47.450 --> 00:21:49.780
And we already
talked about why you

00:21:49.780 --> 00:21:52.390
want to go as far up the
management stack as possible,

00:21:52.390 --> 00:21:58.840
so Serverless is a great place
to do these sorts of simple web

00:21:58.840 --> 00:22:00.580
hooks.

00:22:00.580 --> 00:22:03.100
I'm actually going to use
both Cloud Functions and App

00:22:03.100 --> 00:22:06.100
Engine in this talk, because I
wanted to use the GA products

00:22:06.100 --> 00:22:10.030
and show you what I would
recommend for building things

00:22:10.030 --> 00:22:11.488
today.

00:22:11.488 --> 00:22:13.030
With Cloud Run, I
could have put this

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:15.160
all in a single
container, which has

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:17.740
some nice advantages in
terms of managing modules

00:22:17.740 --> 00:22:20.840
and things like that.

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:24.365
So Serverless--
other people have

00:22:24.365 --> 00:22:26.615
said this-- it's like a
high-level language for cloud.

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:35.990
Some of you have probably
been from basic, or C,

00:22:35.990 --> 00:22:39.080
up to object-oriented
languages where

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.060
you can get a lot
of encapsulation

00:22:41.060 --> 00:22:44.960
and remove a lot of
duplication from your code.

00:22:44.960 --> 00:22:47.390
And Serverless is the
same sort of thing.

00:22:47.390 --> 00:22:49.220
You get a lot of stuff for free.

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:50.960
You get scaling and pay-per-use.

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:53.090
And that includes
scale down to zero.

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:54.710
So when you're not
using it, you pay

00:22:54.710 --> 00:22:57.320
nothing, which is great for
development environments

00:22:57.320 --> 00:23:03.458
because, probably, if
I were paying for this,

00:23:03.458 --> 00:23:05.000
rather than having
Google pay for it,

00:23:05.000 --> 00:23:07.820
it would have cost me somewhere
between free and $0.25

00:23:07.820 --> 00:23:10.130
to do all the development work.

00:23:10.130 --> 00:23:14.640
in terms of serving, obviously,
my hours are more than that.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.280
You also get a lot
of stuff for free,

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:18.950
like SSL I mentioned
earlier in network routing.

00:23:18.950 --> 00:23:24.440
So App Engine allows you to
roll out versions incrementally.

00:23:24.440 --> 00:23:26.870
That functionality is
coming for Cloud Run.

00:23:26.870 --> 00:23:32.210
And there's some similar things
you can do with Functions.

00:23:32.210 --> 00:23:34.790
It steers you towards a
bunch of best practices

00:23:34.790 --> 00:23:37.460
that your IT
department will love.

00:23:37.460 --> 00:23:41.150
Stateless applications
are way easier to handle.

00:23:41.150 --> 00:23:43.760
You have a problem, you can roll
back to the previous version.

00:23:43.760 --> 00:23:45.040
It's super fast.

00:23:45.040 --> 00:23:46.760
You will almost always
solve your issue,

00:23:46.760 --> 00:23:49.250
if the application
was at fault. You

00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:51.795
don't have to worry about,
oh, what about customers

00:23:51.795 --> 00:23:52.920
who are on the new version?

00:23:52.920 --> 00:23:55.980
You can just move them back.

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:59.840
Similarly, we take care of OS
patches, access control tools,

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:03.050
and things like that, a
bunch of other problems

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:05.480
that you probably
prefer not to have.

00:24:05.480 --> 00:24:10.010
And basically, it's just
your code all the time.

00:24:10.010 --> 00:24:12.500
Now, there are some cases--

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:14.757
not so much for Dialogflow,
because Dialogflow

00:24:14.757 --> 00:24:17.090
is very carefully designed
to let you be a stateless web

00:24:17.090 --> 00:24:17.840
app--

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:19.730
but if you're
integrating directly

00:24:19.730 --> 00:24:21.680
with Actions on Google,
or you were building

00:24:21.680 --> 00:24:26.330
some other types of things, if
you're keeping local storage,

00:24:26.330 --> 00:24:29.120
like a session cache,
or something like that,

00:24:29.120 --> 00:24:32.128
Serverless is not
the place to go.

00:24:32.128 --> 00:24:33.920
You need to put that
in an external service

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:36.770
because, all of a sudden,
you have five instances.

00:24:36.770 --> 00:24:38.778
And sessions are
bouncing between them.

00:24:38.778 --> 00:24:40.820
And people are forgetting
that they're logged in.

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:42.690
And everybody's sad.

00:24:42.690 --> 00:24:45.060
And your individual
instances aren't addressable.

00:24:45.060 --> 00:24:47.300
So if you're thinking
of doing something

00:24:47.300 --> 00:24:50.780
fancy and complicated, you don't
know how many instances you're

00:24:50.780 --> 00:24:53.930
going to have, and you can't
control where the requests go.

00:24:53.930 --> 00:25:00.130
So in this particular demo, I
have the Assistant hooked up

00:25:00.130 --> 00:25:01.270
to Dialogflow.

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:04.690
And then using Cloud Functions
to service that web hook,

00:25:04.690 --> 00:25:07.060
because it's a single request.

00:25:07.060 --> 00:25:09.340
I also have a
management interface

00:25:09.340 --> 00:25:11.290
that's on App Engine,
that's multiple pages.

00:25:11.290 --> 00:25:14.620
It is a little web
application where

00:25:14.620 --> 00:25:17.822
you can see all of your
orders, and on a different page

00:25:17.822 --> 00:25:19.780
where the chefs can see
what they need to make,

00:25:19.780 --> 00:25:25.570
because I'm making a
application for ordering food--

00:25:25.570 --> 00:25:27.810
because goodness
knows, there's not

00:25:27.810 --> 00:25:30.700
enough ways to order food yet.

00:25:30.700 --> 00:25:33.120
And in the back
end, everything is

00:25:33.120 --> 00:25:35.190
being stored in Cloud
Firestore, which

00:25:35.190 --> 00:25:38.340
has a interesting
functionality where,

00:25:38.340 --> 00:25:39.840
if you're in Firestore
Mode, you can

00:25:39.840 --> 00:25:42.210
set a trigger on
certain objects that

00:25:42.210 --> 00:25:45.210
will call a cloud function
every time they change.

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.467
And so every time
the order changes,

00:25:47.467 --> 00:25:49.050
I have a chance to
view it and decide,

00:25:49.050 --> 00:25:51.570
is it time to send it off to
external payment processing?

00:25:51.570 --> 00:25:57.090
Is there other work, like
going back and recomputing

00:25:57.090 --> 00:25:59.580
the total price of the
order that I should do?

00:25:59.580 --> 00:26:01.315
Because look, it just changed.

00:26:03.890 --> 00:26:06.020
And it's a cute way--

00:26:06.020 --> 00:26:08.060
this pattern with triggers,
and then going back

00:26:08.060 --> 00:26:13.700
to the same Firestore is a cute
way of building a little state

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:17.420
machine where you don't need
to separately use something

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:20.000
like Pub/Sub and your
database and think carefully

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:23.438
about how to manage the
transactions between the two.

00:26:23.438 --> 00:26:25.730
And so all of these components,
including the database,

00:26:25.730 --> 00:26:29.120
actually are Serverless
because Firestore

00:26:29.120 --> 00:26:32.880
will scale up to thousands
of requests per second,

00:26:32.880 --> 00:26:33.710
if you need it.

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:37.195
And again, if you're storing
very little or no data,

00:26:37.195 --> 00:26:39.320
it costs you nothing because
it's in the free tier.

00:26:39.320 --> 00:26:40.570
You pay only for what you use.

00:26:44.140 --> 00:26:52.340
And so, this is where I'm going
to switch over and show you--

00:26:52.340 --> 00:26:58.320
just a moment-- show you
the simulator for Actions

00:26:58.320 --> 00:26:59.950
on Google.

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:02.010
So this is the way
that you would actually

00:27:02.010 --> 00:27:06.180
go to develop your application.

00:27:06.180 --> 00:27:09.130
When you're in test mode, it
has a nice user interface.

00:27:09.130 --> 00:27:12.720
You can also link
it to your phone.

00:27:12.720 --> 00:27:16.650
You can see up here that
there is an option for Testing

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:17.160
On Device.

00:27:17.160 --> 00:27:19.890
And I have it enabled, so I
can also try testing things

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:20.520
with a phone.

00:27:20.520 --> 00:27:26.550
It's a little faster for testing
to just be able to type here.

00:27:26.550 --> 00:27:28.290
And it has nice suggestions.

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:32.670
So I can say, talk
to the demo shop,

00:27:32.670 --> 00:27:35.560
and it should get back
to me after a moment.

00:27:35.560 --> 00:27:36.960
The other great
thing is that you

00:27:36.960 --> 00:27:40.500
can see, for debugging,
all of the requests

00:27:40.500 --> 00:27:44.785
that are getting sent and the
responses that are coming back.

00:27:44.785 --> 00:27:47.160
So you can see my code said,
"It's a great day for demo."

00:27:49.900 --> 00:27:51.630
So let's see what's on the menu.

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:57.230
And we have three items.

00:27:57.230 --> 00:28:01.070
This is being pulled
dynamically from the database.

00:28:01.070 --> 00:28:05.870
Again, I didn't really
want to spend a lot of time

00:28:05.870 --> 00:28:08.550
coming up with cute data.

00:28:08.550 --> 00:28:11.930
So you can see that
we have three dishes,

00:28:11.930 --> 00:28:14.560
and it's just fetching these.

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:16.120
Let me make that bigger for you.

00:28:22.720 --> 00:28:24.870
And so it's going through
these three dishes.

00:28:24.870 --> 00:28:33.440
And then you can say, I'd
like to start an order.

00:28:33.440 --> 00:28:35.210
And it will go.

00:28:35.210 --> 00:28:38.630
And actually, you
can see up here

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:42.760
that I have sandbox
transactions.

00:28:42.760 --> 00:28:45.510
And if this was the first
time I was using it,

00:28:45.510 --> 00:28:49.950
I would have to actually allow
the app to access my payment

00:28:49.950 --> 00:28:52.185
information.

00:28:52.185 --> 00:28:54.060
And that actually has
to be done on a device,

00:28:54.060 --> 00:28:55.530
not on the simulator.

00:28:58.410 --> 00:29:01.010
So there's a couple of things
that you can't do here.

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:04.310
You can't actually place a
payment through the simulator,

00:29:04.310 --> 00:29:06.320
but it's really
helpful for debugging

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.240
everything but the last stage.

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:12.860
So can we switch back
to the slides now?

00:29:15.840 --> 00:29:18.780
OK, so a little
bit about how I've

00:29:18.780 --> 00:29:22.380
laid out data in Firestore.

00:29:22.380 --> 00:29:23.940
If you haven't
used it, Firestore

00:29:23.940 --> 00:29:26.520
is a document database.

00:29:26.520 --> 00:29:28.410
So it holds collections
of documents

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:29.760
under different names.

00:29:29.760 --> 00:29:36.850
And an individual document
can have further collections

00:29:36.850 --> 00:29:37.350
under it.

00:29:37.350 --> 00:29:40.080
So you can see under
Salads, we have

00:29:40.080 --> 00:29:43.660
a bunch of different choices,
like what greens do you want?

00:29:43.660 --> 00:29:46.450
And, do you want avocado
for an extra charge?

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:48.780
And we have a separate
collection that is orders.

00:29:48.780 --> 00:29:53.270
And then inside
each order, there's

00:29:53.270 --> 00:29:56.910
dishes, a list of what
type it is, and then

00:29:56.910 --> 00:30:00.120
what items you've chosen to
include, and the total price.

00:30:03.000 --> 00:30:06.090
And then there's a whole
collection of orders.

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:09.990
And we have Cloud
Functions set up

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:13.560
to listen on those orders,
which is actually kind of fun

00:30:13.560 --> 00:30:17.100
when you're using the
development viewer.

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:18.690
Because you can
change values, and you

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:22.410
can see the live updates as your
background function comes in,

00:30:22.410 --> 00:30:26.430
and it fixes things
or adjusts them.

00:30:26.430 --> 00:30:28.530
And when I was writing
code and I discovered

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:30.900
I had bad values for some
of the computed stuff,

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:32.670
I could just go
in and delete it.

00:30:32.670 --> 00:30:35.850
That caused the function
to reevaluate and create

00:30:35.850 --> 00:30:37.980
the correct data.

00:30:37.980 --> 00:30:41.940
In terms of Dialogflow,
can we switch back

00:30:41.940 --> 00:30:44.190
to the demo machine?

00:30:44.190 --> 00:30:45.870
I'm going to give
you a lot of practice

00:30:45.870 --> 00:30:48.940
switching back and forth.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:56.570
So those of you who haven't
used it before, Dialogflow

00:30:56.570 --> 00:30:59.120
has a bunch of--

00:30:59.120 --> 00:31:02.390
so this is the Show Me a Menu.

00:31:02.390 --> 00:31:06.140
And you enter a variety
of training phrases.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:10.940
And let's look at a more
interesting item now.

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:16.130
So the Add Intent
lets you say, hey,

00:31:16.130 --> 00:31:18.880
I want to get a salad,
or something like that.

00:31:18.880 --> 00:31:24.850
And you give it a list of these
entities that it might fill

00:31:24.850 --> 00:31:28.960
in-- so salad, and
bowls, or whatever--

00:31:28.960 --> 00:31:31.420
I was feeling healthy
when I got started--

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:35.440
are all filled in.

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:38.010
And you give it several
different training phrases

00:31:38.010 --> 00:31:42.460
that it can learn where
those foods might be.

00:31:42.460 --> 00:31:44.880
So this is great, if you
like having a UI to keep

00:31:44.880 --> 00:31:45.880
track of all this stuff.

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:48.130
But for a more
production environment,

00:31:48.130 --> 00:31:55.480
you may want to, instead,
export this as a zip file.

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:58.260
So if you go over
here to Export,

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:06.060
you can export this as a zip
file, and then import it.

00:32:06.060 --> 00:32:11.510
And you can see here that
here are the different things

00:32:11.510 --> 00:32:14.810
that a user might say.

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:17.130
I want a salad.

00:32:17.130 --> 00:32:18.440
It's a dish.

00:32:18.440 --> 00:32:23.100
It's the Dish entity, and
it's defined by a user.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.010
Hey, can I get another thing?

00:32:26.010 --> 00:32:31.420
So having the UI is nice for
creating these to begin with,

00:32:31.420 --> 00:32:33.610
but you can then actually
download this and check it

00:32:33.610 --> 00:32:37.270
into source control, and
then create a zip file

00:32:37.270 --> 00:32:39.730
and restore it to
load things back up.

00:32:39.730 --> 00:32:43.140
So if you're doing
develop in one place,

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.220
doing a production
elsewhere, this

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:49.620
is a great tool for
seeing what's going on.

00:32:49.620 --> 00:32:51.960
Can we go back to the slides?

00:32:51.960 --> 00:32:56.280
I'm going to give you folks
a workout on the video.

00:32:56.280 --> 00:33:00.023
So there's various
different items

00:33:00.023 --> 00:33:01.440
that the web hook
can return with.

00:33:01.440 --> 00:33:04.760
So one thing that
they can do is just,

00:33:04.760 --> 00:33:06.080
hey, here's some more text.

00:33:06.080 --> 00:33:08.690
You can also say
something like, hey, I

00:33:08.690 --> 00:33:11.207
want this person to
start a transaction

00:33:11.207 --> 00:33:12.290
or complete a transaction.

00:33:12.290 --> 00:33:14.295
So starting a
transaction says, please

00:33:14.295 --> 00:33:16.670
make sure that they've got
all their payment information,

00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:18.410
and they're willing
to share it with you.

00:33:18.410 --> 00:33:20.705
Completing the transaction
gives them actually,

00:33:20.705 --> 00:33:22.580
this is what your total
price is going to be.

00:33:22.580 --> 00:33:26.150
Do you want to pay it or not?

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:28.160
And you can also set
context information

00:33:28.160 --> 00:33:30.030
that will be carried
along to the future.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:34.300
So when you say, I'd
like to place an order,

00:33:34.300 --> 00:33:36.740
the demo creates an
order in Firestore

00:33:36.740 --> 00:33:40.070
and then puts that
into Dialogflow

00:33:40.070 --> 00:33:42.900
so that the next time
you make a request,

00:33:42.900 --> 00:33:46.340
the next web hook
knows what that is.

00:33:46.340 --> 00:33:48.300
And let's see.

00:33:48.300 --> 00:33:48.800
Yes.

00:33:48.800 --> 00:33:53.370
So this is what the
web hook looks like.

00:33:53.370 --> 00:33:57.770
So you get a single request,
and it tells you which

00:33:57.770 --> 00:33:59.330
of those different intents is.

00:33:59.330 --> 00:34:02.280
And each of those is
basically a separate function.

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:05.600
So in Node, there's
a nice wrapper that

00:34:05.600 --> 00:34:09.889
lets you map these, just
annotate these names

00:34:09.889 --> 00:34:12.020
being different functions.

00:34:12.020 --> 00:34:14.840
In Python, it's really easy
to write a little wrapper that

00:34:14.840 --> 00:34:17.310
does the same thing.

00:34:17.310 --> 00:34:23.690
So that's what we have here,
is just a quick dispatch loop.

00:34:23.690 --> 00:34:26.750
And whenever you want
to add a new function,

00:34:26.750 --> 00:34:28.639
you write the function,
and then just add it

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:33.409
to handlers, along with the
mapping to those Dialogflow.

00:34:36.620 --> 00:34:40.550
And for local
development and testing,

00:34:40.550 --> 00:34:45.440
you can use flask-ngrok and get
a much faster development cycle

00:34:45.440 --> 00:34:47.870
and get interactive
debugging when

00:34:47.870 --> 00:34:49.639
you're getting things at setup.

00:34:49.639 --> 00:34:53.030
So ngrok is one of
several services

00:34:53.030 --> 00:34:55.545
that will run a
endpoint on the web,

00:34:55.545 --> 00:34:57.170
and then proxy it to
your local machine

00:34:57.170 --> 00:34:58.760
if you're behind a firewall.

00:34:58.760 --> 00:35:04.100
So that was how I did a lot
of the initial iteration.

00:35:04.100 --> 00:35:06.980
I'll also show you how I
do it with Cloud Functions.

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:11.140
Cloud Functions takes
a little while--

00:35:11.140 --> 00:35:16.140
30-ish seconds or so, most of
the time for this Python app.

00:35:16.140 --> 00:35:21.310
So it's a little bit slower,
but not terribly slow.

00:35:21.310 --> 00:35:25.700
But it's sometimes nice to
have that instantaneous loop.

00:35:25.700 --> 00:35:27.720
And can we switch back
to the demo machine?

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:31.310
And I will show what
this code looks like.

00:35:31.310 --> 00:35:36.740
And maybe we will see about--

00:35:36.740 --> 00:35:41.420
let's see, in main--

00:35:41.420 --> 00:35:42.125
well, let's see.

00:35:44.960 --> 00:35:50.130
We can-- let's see.

00:35:50.130 --> 00:35:51.730
That's still a builder.

00:35:51.730 --> 00:35:59.650
So where we say we have
so-and-so items on the menu,

00:35:59.650 --> 00:36:05.776
we can sed chain this
to, it's a surprise.

00:36:08.730 --> 00:36:14.270
And comment these others out, so
we don't have to ask as often.

00:36:14.270 --> 00:36:23.760
And then the deploy
command looks something

00:36:23.760 --> 00:36:25.560
like this down at the bottom.

00:36:25.560 --> 00:36:28.560
Deploy the voice
function triggered by HP.

00:36:28.560 --> 00:36:34.290
It's the Python 3.7 runtime,
so that's pretty much

00:36:34.290 --> 00:36:36.990
the latest version of Python.

00:36:36.990 --> 00:36:40.260
And it'll take a moment
or two to upload.

00:36:40.260 --> 00:36:43.950
And you can go over--

00:36:43.950 --> 00:36:47.100
and this is still working.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:51.030
Can I get a salad?

00:36:51.030 --> 00:36:53.410
After about 20 minutes, it'll
time out your interaction.

00:36:53.410 --> 00:36:58.560
So we will get started again.

00:36:58.560 --> 00:37:03.210
And there's is some
built-in chit-chat

00:37:03.210 --> 00:37:05.100
that Google ships
with it that gives you

00:37:05.100 --> 00:37:08.713
a few suggestions for stuff
that you can leave on, or not.

00:37:08.713 --> 00:37:10.380
There's one that's
like, tell me a joke,

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:12.940
that you might or might want.

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:15.580
So this is still building.

00:37:15.580 --> 00:37:19.480
It says it might take
up to two minutes.

00:37:19.480 --> 00:37:22.980
The other thing that you can
do while this is going on

00:37:22.980 --> 00:37:28.830
is you can look at
the existing logs.

00:37:28.830 --> 00:37:32.950
And you can see we
just made this request.

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:34.385
This is the Welcome intent.

00:37:34.385 --> 00:37:35.760
This is all logging
that I added.

00:37:38.430 --> 00:37:44.558
And here, we're responding
with some text that says,

00:37:44.558 --> 00:37:45.725
it's a great day for a demo.

00:37:48.870 --> 00:37:53.940
And now, it looks like we
have updated the function.

00:37:53.940 --> 00:37:56.780
So let's see what's on the menu.

00:38:01.940 --> 00:38:03.820
It has not updated yet.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:09.210
What's on the menu?

00:38:12.160 --> 00:38:13.900
It's a surprise.

00:38:13.900 --> 00:38:16.030
So it took a moment.

00:38:16.030 --> 00:38:18.310
So that's sort of
the iteration cycle

00:38:18.310 --> 00:38:20.800
when you're working
in the cloud.

00:38:20.800 --> 00:38:23.900
Obviously, you can
still work pretty fast.

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:27.150
But if you have Flask
reloading on local changes,

00:38:27.150 --> 00:38:33.210
it's almost instant, although
it can be hard to read the logs.

00:38:33.210 --> 00:38:36.800
I actually find the log viewer
to be easier to pick things

00:38:36.800 --> 00:38:38.925
out than when you just get
a whole bunch of output.

00:38:42.645 --> 00:38:48.980
And so yeah, this is--

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:49.480
yeah.

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:52.270
And we're checking the user
as well because, at one point,

00:38:52.270 --> 00:38:54.590
we said hello to the person.

00:38:54.590 --> 00:38:56.320
So I could also--

00:38:56.320 --> 00:38:59.170
you'll see here there's a
little bit of integration

00:38:59.170 --> 00:39:00.320
with Google Identity.

00:39:00.320 --> 00:39:03.250
So you can get the
user's name and so forth,

00:39:03.250 --> 00:39:05.980
if they've given
you that permission.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:10.200
I set this up already,
so it's there.

00:39:10.200 --> 00:39:13.224
Can you switch me back
to the slides, please?

00:39:13.224 --> 00:39:15.460
Let's see.

00:39:15.460 --> 00:39:20.400
So we already talked
about most of this.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:23.370
In a minute, I will probably
walk through this back end

00:39:23.370 --> 00:39:24.910
processing piece.

00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:29.050
But this is basically
what it looks like.

00:39:29.050 --> 00:39:30.640
That Validate
method is where you

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:35.560
put all your business logic for,
oh, hey, is the price right?

00:39:35.560 --> 00:39:40.030
We should update it, extract
all the fields, and so forth.

00:39:40.030 --> 00:39:43.030
The important thing to
note is this last block

00:39:43.030 --> 00:39:47.290
at the bottom that says,
if these two documents

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:49.270
are different, then set them.

00:39:49.270 --> 00:39:50.710
Otherwise, don't.

00:39:50.710 --> 00:39:53.830
If you don't do that
differencing part at the end,

00:39:53.830 --> 00:39:56.160
and you just always do
an unconditional set--

00:39:56.160 --> 00:39:58.930
it's Serverless, it will scale.

00:39:58.930 --> 00:40:01.010
And you have created
an infinite loop.

00:40:01.010 --> 00:40:04.840
So we can scale
that pretty fast.

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:07.010
Your build scales with usage.

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:11.950
So if you're doing this
pattern of writing back

00:40:11.950 --> 00:40:14.020
to the same document,
you may want

00:40:14.020 --> 00:40:16.390
to put it in a few safety
checks that you're not

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:19.360
going to overwhelm the
database in a spin loop.

00:40:21.920 --> 00:40:24.800
So in my particular
case, it made more sense

00:40:24.800 --> 00:40:27.278
to treat this as, let's
check a condition.

00:40:27.278 --> 00:40:28.320
Let's fix that condition.

00:40:28.320 --> 00:40:30.020
Check the condition,
fix the condition.

00:40:30.020 --> 00:40:32.430
But you could also have a
field that was a state machine.

00:40:32.430 --> 00:40:39.540
So order received, we've
created your appointment,

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:44.360
we've sent a follow-up email
to ask you what kind of car

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:49.310
do you have, we've sent
you a suggestion of tires,

00:40:49.310 --> 00:40:53.150
we've got the tires ordered, and
now everything's ready to go.

00:40:55.870 --> 00:41:01.140
And then the last piece is
a status web application.

00:41:01.140 --> 00:41:05.040
So it's great being
able to place orders

00:41:05.040 --> 00:41:07.260
through a chat interface.

00:41:07.260 --> 00:41:09.238
Maybe you're in the car
or something like that,

00:41:09.238 --> 00:41:11.280
[INAUDIBLE] want to be
able to see, hey, what are

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:12.990
all the last orders that I had?

00:41:12.990 --> 00:41:16.290
And so voice isn't
going to replace

00:41:16.290 --> 00:41:19.700
those sorts of interfaces,
it's going to add to them.

00:41:19.700 --> 00:41:24.070
And some things will make
more sense to do in a web UI.

00:41:24.070 --> 00:41:29.820
So if you can flip back
to the demo machine again,

00:41:29.820 --> 00:41:34.920
you can see my beautiful
web design skills.

00:41:34.920 --> 00:41:39.000
And this is a history
of all of the orders

00:41:39.000 --> 00:41:40.740
that you've completed.

00:41:40.740 --> 00:41:45.010
And to show how a little bit
of this reconciliation loop

00:41:45.010 --> 00:41:52.900
works, if we go over
and look at the orders,

00:41:52.900 --> 00:41:55.022
I've done this with a
couple of different--

00:41:55.022 --> 00:42:01.870
So this is that last
order in the list.

00:42:01.870 --> 00:42:07.380
And if I decide to change
the value, I can do that.

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:10.223
And over here, it
will tell me I paid--

00:42:10.223 --> 00:42:19.438
ah, that's not-- are
there two of these?

00:42:29.410 --> 00:42:34.720
Well, so my web scales
are apparently really bad.

00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:37.790
If I force reload to that--

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:39.170
It has cash $15 in.

00:42:39.170 --> 00:42:43.170
I apparently don't know
how to do cash busting.

00:42:43.170 --> 00:42:48.280
I'm a back end engineer,
so this was all fun for me.

00:42:48.280 --> 00:42:52.570
But the fun thing
that you can do here

00:42:52.570 --> 00:42:57.730
is if you delete the
price, for example, you

00:42:57.730 --> 00:43:00.850
will see it pop back up in
just a moment, added again.

00:43:00.850 --> 00:43:04.463
That's that background
process recognizing, hey,

00:43:04.463 --> 00:43:05.630
there's not a total on here.

00:43:05.630 --> 00:43:08.110
I should go compute it.

00:43:08.110 --> 00:43:13.900
And the actual code
looks pretty simple.

00:43:17.240 --> 00:43:20.660
If we don't have a
total, just update it.

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:24.830
If it's not done and we got
a payment token from Stripe,

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:26.990
this has a sleep
because I didn't finish

00:43:26.990 --> 00:43:31.645
integrating with Stripes API.

00:43:40.150 --> 00:43:43.090
The other pattern
for this is if you

00:43:43.090 --> 00:43:45.520
have some data you
don't want to check in,

00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:49.190
you can have a config.

00:43:49.190 --> 00:43:53.230
And this cuts off the
right-hand side intentionally,

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:55.390
because I don't want
you to see my full keys.

00:43:55.390 --> 00:43:57.580
But I can store
some keys in here,

00:43:57.580 --> 00:43:59.740
and then load them on
every application request

00:43:59.740 --> 00:44:05.200
without needing to write that
down in a config file that gets

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:06.670
checked in to get.

00:44:06.670 --> 00:44:10.160
Instead, I project them on
the screen for everybody.

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:13.850
But they're cut off, as I said.

00:44:13.850 --> 00:44:17.153
But yes, here's an
object in your database

00:44:17.153 --> 00:44:18.320
that stores all your config.

00:44:18.320 --> 00:44:20.278
It's really handy for
development-- development

00:44:20.278 --> 00:44:23.730
versus production environments.

00:44:23.730 --> 00:44:27.540
And Firestore is a really
good place for this,

00:44:27.540 --> 00:44:31.140
because it lets you have just
sort of amorphous objects

00:44:31.140 --> 00:44:33.520
where you can add another
key if you need to.

00:44:33.520 --> 00:44:41.120
So if I discovered I needed an
extra key, it can be a string.

00:44:41.120 --> 00:44:46.820
Or I can even have a
JavaScript map and nest things.

00:44:50.194 --> 00:44:52.470
And so if you can switch
me back to the slides.

00:44:58.140 --> 00:45:00.220
I've put a debugging
view on that's

00:45:00.220 --> 00:45:01.470
basically a view for the chef.

00:45:01.470 --> 00:45:02.970
It looks a lot like
that other page,

00:45:02.970 --> 00:45:07.140
except that it has all the items
in a little bit more detail,

00:45:07.140 --> 00:45:10.810
like which ingredients
you should have.

00:45:10.810 --> 00:45:15.880
And you also get a lot
of these debugging tools.

00:45:15.880 --> 00:45:18.300
So you saw the log viewer.

00:45:18.300 --> 00:45:20.240
Stackdriver also has
a trace mechanism,

00:45:20.240 --> 00:45:28.190
so you can see why is my app
slow, and frequency, latency.

00:45:28.190 --> 00:45:30.370
There's also request
tracing through the system.

00:45:30.370 --> 00:45:34.510
So you can say, oh,
hey, I called Firestore

00:45:34.510 --> 00:45:35.960
eight times it turns out.

00:45:35.960 --> 00:45:39.190
And that's why everything is
slow is because I should really

00:45:39.190 --> 00:45:42.280
consolidate that down
to two database calls.

00:45:42.280 --> 00:45:45.950
And Serverless gives you a lot
of these batteries included,

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:48.310
so there's only a small
amount of work to integrate.

00:45:48.310 --> 00:45:50.920
Whereas, if you're
running on a VM,

00:45:50.920 --> 00:45:52.750
you might have a
much larger list of,

00:45:52.750 --> 00:45:54.310
get the monitoring
agent installed,

00:45:54.310 --> 00:45:56.470
figure out what
credentials it needs,

00:45:56.470 --> 00:45:59.560
figure out which monitoring
agent you're going to use.

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:02.940
And so Serverless has picked
a lot of this stuff for you.

00:46:02.940 --> 00:46:08.340
And so this is a
bunch of information.

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:13.790
But before we get
to the Q&amp;A, I would

00:46:13.790 --> 00:46:17.060
love to get your
feedback on this session.

